{:edb/db-before-t 5254 :edb/db-after-t 5255 :edb/tx-data [[13194139538567 50 #inst "2026-10-21T01:02:51.655Z" 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186054625 1038 #edb/ref 17592186670786 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186054625 1038 #edb/ref 17592186670787 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186054625 1038 #edb/ref 17592186670788 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186054625 1040 #edb/ref 17592186608381 13194139538567 17592186576411 false] [17592186054625 1040 #edb/ref 17592186670785 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186608363 1173 true 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186608368 1173 true 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186608371 1173 true 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186608372 1074 "First reorder the terms so the two positive logarithms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)-2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)-2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)-2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$." 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186608372 1074 "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &= -\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we combine the factors with the same sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(\\sqrt{z}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{2}\\left(y^{3}\\sqrt{z}\\right)-\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& -2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{2}\\left(z\\right)+3\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{2}\\left(\\frac{y^{3}\\sqrt{z}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$." 13194139538567 17592186576411 false] [17592186608381 1168 "generators/d2/d22bc61dbe5d5e954a0b7cce9d800e9795e2ef28137adc1a72a2db643e6feafd/generator.py" 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186670767 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)" 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186670767 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186670767 1083 #uuid "052d61ab-849c-5821-a4c8-7b7df8edf4c1" 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [17592186670768 1080 "\\log\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)+\\log\\left(z\\right)" 13194139538567 17592186576411 true] [175921866707